{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Behold, if you do well, lift up.</b> An expression of gift, as in 'וישא משאת מאת פניו' (and lifted up a gift from before him), meaning: <b>if you do well</b> — the gift, that you bring again another gift, it is accepted, well and good; and if you do not do well the gift, <b>at the door sin crouches</b> — at the door of your words, the sin — guard, that you will not be able to acquit yourself with words. And this verse is among the abbreviated expressions, like 'לכן כל הורג קין' (therefore whoever kills Cain), 'כל מכה יבוסי' (every striking Jebusite), 'וגע בצנור' (and touch the trough). Another interpretation: <b>if you do well</b> — that you conquer your inclination from killing your brother, whom you envy — I have it to lift your iniquities, an expression of 'נושא עון' (bearing iniquity). <b>And if you do not do well</b>, that you do not conquer your inclination — <b>at the door</b> of your house, the punishment <b>crouches. Sin</b> — an expression of punishment, as in 'אם יקרך עון בדבר זה' (if an offense should befall you in this matter), which is said regarding one who consults a necromancer; and you are able to kill him if you wish. And if you say: he will flee from me and I will not be able to kill him — it says <b>and to you is his desire</b>: that of Abel your brother, to be whole with you as at first, and he will not flee from you. And if you say: he is stronger than I — <b>and you shall rule over him</b>, meaning you will overpower him and be able to kill him. That is: 'And Cain said to Abel his brother' — that he should rule over him, as HaShem had told him, but Abel did not agree to the matter; therefore 'And Cain rose up against Abel his brother and killed him.'" "<b>And to you is his desire.</b> Rashi explained: he (the evil inclination) craves and yearns to cause you to stumble. And if you say: since this is so, you will not be punished for it — it says <b>and you shall rule over him</b>: over the evil inclination. Another interpretation: <b>and you shall rule over him</b> — over your brother. By right of the firstborn's inheritance, for you are the firstborn. And there is an aggadah in Breshit Rabbah: about what were they disputing? One said to the other: Come, let us divide the world. Abel took the movable property and Cain took the land. And thus it is written: 'Now Abel was a keeper of sheep, and Cain was a tiller of the ground.' Cain said: 'The land on which you stand is mine.' And Abel said: 'What you are wearing is mine.' This one said: Strip! and that one said: Fly off! From this: 'And Cain rose up against Abel,' etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The word שאת in the lemma is read two ways: first as 'gift' (from נשיאת משאת), then in the second interpretation as 'I will lift/bear (your iniquity).' The phrase דגבי בעלת אוב introduces the prooftext from Exodus 21:22 (lit. 'which is said regarding one who consults a necromancer'), apparently as the context where עון means punishment.", 1 "The Aramaic phrases (ארעא דאת קאים עלה דידי הוא; מה דאת לביש דידי הוא; דין אמר חלוץ ודין אמר פרח) are translated literally: 'the land you stand on is mine'; 'what you are wearing is mine'; 'this one said: strip, and that one said: fly off (i.e., leave).'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:04.513016534Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}